The linked article discusses how the 2027 budget proposal would curb federal payments for scientific publishing and reduce funding for many US institutions.

Exactly. A large reason why LLMs are detectable is that they have preferences instilled through training data and reinforcement learning. Asking a model to rewrite something gives the LLM an opportunity to apply its own preferences to your text. Sometimes these preferences are helpful, like proper grammar and spelling, but other times they actively erase the author's intent and voice.

This can manifest in softening language to align statements with what the LLM is comfortable with, replacing the author's metaphors with the LLM's preferred ones, and altering the author's voice—such as their tics, sentence structure, and vocabulary choice—to a more 'default' voice the LLM prefers.

@max_spero_: "I say this all the time, but a large reason why LLMs are detectable is they have preferences instilled into them through training data and RL."

Anthropic said Mythos was too dangerous to release, but then four random guys in a Discord gained access on day one by guessing the URL. This is pretty insane: a group in a private Discord guessed the endpoint from Anthropic's naming conventions, which they figured out from the leak in the Mercor breach three weeks ago. They used a contractor's legitimate evaluation credentials to walk in and have been using it ever since to build simple websites.

The AI that finds zero-days in every operating system on earth was defeated by address bar autocomplete. 

@business: "Anthropic's Mythos has been accessed by a small group of unauthorized users, raising questions about control of the AI model."

